<subsection xml:id="subsec-program-layout">
  <title>Program Layout</title>
  <p>
	Notice how the program is  neatly arranged on the page.
	This is done deliberately so that the program is easy to read and understand.
  </p>
  <p>
	In Java, program elements may be arranged in any way the programmer likes. However, the fact that the rules governing the layout of the program 
	are so lax makes it all the more important that we adopt a good programming style; one that will help make programs easy to read.
  </p>
  <p>
	So look at how things are presented in <c>HelloWorld</c> (<xref ref="lstng_helloworld"></xref>). 
        Notice how beginning and ending braces, { and }, are aligned.
	Braces are used to mark the beginning and end of different blocks of code (blocks are explained below)
        in a Java program and it can sometimes be difficult to know which beginning and end braces are matched up.
	Proper indentation and the use of single-line comments (also explained below) make it easier to determine how the braces are matched up.
  </p>
  <p>
	Notice how indentation is used to show when one element of the program is contained within another element. Indeed, the inner braces
	in HelloWorld define a component of the program that is contained within a larger component defined by the outer braces.
	Use of indentation in this way,	to identify the program's structure,	makes the program easier to read and understand.
  </p>
  <principle>
    <title>PROGRAMMING TIP: Use of Indentation</title>
    <p>
	  Indent the code within a block and align the block's opening and closing braces.
	  Optionally use a comment to mark the end of a block of code.
    </p>
  </principle>
</subsection>
